 ## Background
-CM Splatting was introduced as a standard method for surface-aligned splatting in non-photorealistic rendering [S1, unofficial]. It relies on Plane Principal Component Analysis [Rus09] to estimate splat normal vectors and orientations from 3D point data [S1, unofficial]. It was compared against the Householder (HH) formula in a study by Anjos et al. [dARLP17] to evaluate rendering quality on noisy meshes and point clouds [S1, unofficial].
+CM is a standard approach for surface-aligned splatting on clean data, computing surface curvature directions to align splats with minimal curvature [S1, unofficial]. It relies on Plane Principal Component Analysis [Rus09] to estimate splat normal vectors and orientations from 3D point data [S1, unofficial]. It was compared against the Householder (HH) formula in a study by Anjos et al. [dARLP17] to evaluate rendering quality on noisy meshes and point clouds [S1, unofficial].
 
